40,2,1,talk,Mio,,"Wow, I found some really interesting stuff! It looks like we have plenty of help for software, but finding people for field operations is going to be a huge headache. We might be stretched too thin!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,1,talk,George,,"My findings suggest a clear pattern. Our successful exits were lean, back-office software tools. Conversely, the write-off involved heavy physical overhead and manual labor. We should be wary of companies with high operational complexity.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,1,talk,May,,"I've got some sobering news. Our reserves are being drained by follow-on calls much faster than we planned, and our credit line is useless for anything other than physical stock. We're far less liquid than I hoped.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,1,talk,Rin,,"I found something scary! If a company has hardware or depots, the audit takes way longer and costs a lot of money. Also, software companies just tell us their numbers, but we can't actually check them ourselves!","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,1,talk,Mizuki,,My findings confirm that deployment speed varies wildly by sector. Companies dealing with sensitive data or physical hardware face significant delays—sometimes months or even quarters—due to security reviews and supply chain issues.,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,2,talk,Mio,,"@George, that matches what I saw! We have these great logistics advisers just sitting there, but the partners are already swamped with software boards. If we pick another software firm, who's actually going to manage it?","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,2,talk,George,,"@Mio, that is a fair point. However, the data shows that software tools—specifically those for back-office operations—are where we actually see returns. We must not let board capacity drive us toward riskier, hardware-heavy models.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,2,talk,May,,"@George, I hear you on the returns, but we can't ignore the cash flow. If we go for something with high overhead, our current credit line won't save us when the reserves dip further.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,2,talk,Rin,,"@May, exactly! And if we pick something with hardware, the audit will be a total nightmare! I don't want to spend six weeks just waiting for an appraisal. That's way too long!","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@Rin, @May, your concerns align with my findings. The friction isn't just in the audit; it's in the deployment. Hardware and sensitive data sectors create bottlenecks that can stall a company for quarters before they even launch.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Rin"",""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,3,talk,Mio,,"But if we go for hardware just because we have the advisers, we'll be stuck waiting months for recruiters to find field crews! We'll be exhausted before the company even gets off the ground!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,3,talk,George,,"@Mio, we must be careful not to confuse available resources with a viable business model. My research shows that customers are far more loyal to essential tools than to services reliant on manual labor. Stability is paramount.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,3,talk,May,,"@George, stability is great, but it doesn't pay the bills if we're cash-strapped. If we pick a software firm, we need to be sure it doesn't trigger more unexpected follow-on calls that we can't cover.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,3,talk,Rin,,"But wait! If we pick software, we're just trusting their dashboards! I want something we can actually check ourselves so we don't get tricked by numbers that aren't real!","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,3,talk,Mizuki,,"@Rin, while transparency is important, the risk of a total standstill is higher. We cannot ignore that sensitive data and hardware create systemic delays. We should prioritize a model that ensures a fast, predictable path to the first customer.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Rin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Mio,,"I'm starting to feel like we're stuck! Software is fast and stable, but we're out of board seats. Hardware has the advisers, but it's a recruitment and audit nightmare. We need a middle ground!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,2,4,talk,George,,"@Mio, there is no middle ground when it comes to risk. We must prioritize the model that has proven to deliver returns and retain customers. I believe a lean software approach is the only prudent choice.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,2,4,talk,May,,"@George, I can't just agree to ""prudent"" when our liquidity is this fragile. If a lean software firm hits a snag and demands more cash, we're exposed. We need a candidate that doesn't bleed us dry.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,2,4,talk,Rin,,"I don't care if it's lean or not if the numbers are just a mystery! I want a company where we can see the real results ourselves, not just a dashboard that says ""everything is great!""","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,2,4,talk,Mizuki,,"@Rin, @May, we must weigh the risk of ""mystery numbers"" against the risk of a company that simply cannot launch. A dashboard is better than a product stuck in a security review for nine months.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Rin"",""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
60,3,,status,May,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
